:"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-28","committee":"Scrutiny","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Provisional","webcast":false,"url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-29","committee":"Strategy, Finance, Policy & Economic Development","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-30","committee":"Scrutiny","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Provisional","webcast":false,"url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-05","committee":"Audit, Governance and Standards Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-21","committee":"Council","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed; Extraordinary","webcast":false,"url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-05","committee":"Planning Policy Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-18","committee":"Audit, Governance and Standards Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-21","committee":"Strategy, Finance, Policy & Economic Development","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-08-27","committee":"Planning Policy Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-07","committee":"Strategy, Finance, Policy & Economic Development","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-10","committee":"Planning Policy Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Council","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"The \u00a3649k forecast surplus is largely driven by non-recurring treasury management benefits that will not persist.","lead":"A lender assessing the council's financial position needs to understand that the apparent Q1 strength masks a weaker underlying operational position; the surplus will reverse as treasury income normalises to budget levels.","iso":"2026-09-07","paper_title":"Q1 Budget Update","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6289/6289.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Recycling and waste collection costs are rising faster than budgeted due to Norse contract cost increases.","lead":"Waste collection is a statutory service where costs are driven by contractor pricing; this overspend signals inflationary cost pressure that will likely persist through the year and reduce available resources.","iso":"2026-09-07","paper_title":"Q1 Budget Update","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6289/6289.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Homelessness service is forecasting a \u00a3160k cost overspend due to reduced income from temporary accommodation.","lead":"A decline in homelessness service income indicates either reduced ability to charge users or rising need being managed in ways that do not generate revenue. Either way, this creates an unfunded cost pressure on the council.","iso":"2026-09-07","paper_title":"Q1 Budget Update","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6289/6289.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-07","committee":"Strategy, Finance, Policy & Economic Development","paper_title":"Q1 Budget Update","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6289/6289.pdf","headline":"The \u00a3649k forecast surplus is largely driven by non-recurring treasury management benefits that will not persist.","detail":"Of the \u00a3649k surplus, approximately \u00a3500k is treasury interest income running ahead of budget. The council explicitly states this is 'largely non-recurring' and dependent on temporary factors: higher cash balances from prior-year carryforward, capital programme slippage deferring expenditure, and current interest rates. As capital spending accelerates and cash balances normalise, this income benefit will reduce substantially.","category":"other","matters":"A lender assessing the council's financial position needs to understand that the apparent Q1 strength masks a weaker underlying operational position; the surplus will reverse as treasury income normalises to budget levels.","figures":"Treasury interest income forecast \u00a30.5m above budget; total surplus \u00a3649k","quote":"Whilst this additional income provides short-term financial resilience, it should largely be regarded as a one-off benefit rather than a recurring source of funding.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-07","committee":"Strategy, Finance, Policy & Economic Development","paper_title":"Q1 Budget Update","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6289/6289.pdf","headline":"Recycling and waste collection costs are rising faster than budgeted due to Norse contract cost increases.","detail":"The Norse waste collection contract is forecasting a \u00a377k overspend in Q1, specifically attributed to cost inflation within the contract itself rather than volume changes. This signals the council is exposed to contractor cost escalation in a statutory service where pricing is largely outside its control.","category":"overspend","matters":"Waste collection is a statutory service where costs are driven by contractor pricing; this overspend signals inflationary cost pressure that will likely persist through the year and reduce available resources.","figures":"Recycling collection overspend \u00a377k; Environment & Climate Change total overspend \u00a382k","quote":"Recycling collection is forecasting an overspend of \u00a377k attributable to a forecast increase in the Norse contract.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-07","committee":"Strategy, Finance, Policy & Economic Development","paper_title":"Q1 Budget Update","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6289/6289.pdf","headline":"Homelessness service is forecasting a \u00a3160k cost overspend due to reduced income from temporary accommodation.","detail":"The homelessness budget is overspending by \u00a3160k specifically because income from temporary accommodation provision has fallen below budget. This represents a material service cost pressure where expected income is not materialising. The wider Communities, Wellbeing & Housing portfolio shows various underspends that offset this but do not fully compensate.","category":"income","matters":"A decline in homelessness service income indicates either reduced ability to charge users or rising need being managed in ways that do not generate revenue. Either way, this creates an unfunded cost pressure on the council.","figures":"Homelessness overspend \u00a3160k; Communities, Wellbeing & Housing total overspend \u00a389k","quote":"Homelessness is forecasting an overspend of \u00a3160k due to a reduction in income for temporary accommodation.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-21","committee":"Strategy, Finance, Policy & Economic Development","paper_title":"2025/26 Budget Outturn Report","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s5877/5877.pdf","headline":"Capital programme delivered \u00a31.377m underspend, but \u00a30.956m relates to contractually committed work not yet completed.","detail":"Capital outturn was \u00a32.825m against a revised budget of \u00a34.202m. Of the \u00a31.377m variance, \u00a30.956m represents contractually committed expenditure awaiting completion and \u00a30.553m is proposed carry forward. Only \u00a30.132m represents actual overspends on demand-led schemes.","category":"capital","matters":"Shows project delivery timing constraints and procurement challenges, but the commitment is real and spending will follow. Important for understanding capital investment trajectory.","figures":"\u00a31.377m total variance, \u00a30.956m contractually committed, \u00a30.553m carry forward, \u00a30.132m overspends","quote":"Of the \u00a31.377m variance, \u00a30.956m relates to contractually committed expenditure and a further \u00a30.553m is proposed for carry forward into 2026/27 to enable approved projects to be completed.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-21","committee":"Strategy, Finance, Policy & Economic Development","paper_title":"2025/26 Budget Outturn Report","paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s5877/5877.pdf","headline":"External audit fees increased by \u00a3105k, only partially offset by a \u00a346.8k audit grant, signalling rising compliance costs.","detail":"The Strategy, Finance & Policy portfolio recorded \u00a3105k pressure on external audit fees. The council received \u00a346.8k in audit grant funding but still faced a net pressure. This reflects rising external audit costs across the sector.","category":"other","matters":"Rising audit fees reduce financial flexibility and are a structural cost increase with limited council control. They reflect market-wide cost inflation in audit services.","figures":"\u00a3105k pressure, \u00a346.8k grant received, net pressure of approximately \u00a358k","quote":"\u00a3105k pressure on external audit fees, partially offset by a \u00a346.8k audit grant","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-07"},"name":"Rochford","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-21","since_iso":"2026-08-22","distress":false,"headline":"The \u00a3649k forecast surplus is largely driven by non-recurring treasury management benefits that will not persist.","lead":"A lender assessing the council's financial position needs to understand that the apparent Q1 strength masks a weaker underlying operational position; the surplus will reverse as treasury income normalises to budget levels.","also":["2 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Scrutiny on 2026-09-28","n_findings":3,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Q1 Budget Update","top_paper_url":"https://rochford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s6289/6289.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":61,"news_drafts_total":3}}